  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>The peacock bass, Cichla ocellaris, is a large predatory cichlid native to Colombia, Venezuela, Guyana, Suriname, French Guiana and Brazil. Introduced populations exist in several countries, including Singapore and the USA. Often sold as small juveniles, it grows to around 75 cm and needs a tank of at least 300 x 90 x 120 cm as an adult, and it will eat any tankmate small enough to be considered food.</p><ul><li>Needs a tank at least 2 m long</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>Native to northern South America in Guyana, Suriname, French Guiana and Brazil. Introduced populations exist in various countries, including Singapore and the USA.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>Native to northern South America in Guyana, Suriname, French Guiana and Brazil. Introduced populations exist in various countries, including Singapore and the USA.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>More suited to public aquaria, otherwise a very large tank is required. Ultimately, this fish will require a tank of at least 300 x 90 x 120 cm (l x h x w) for an adult fish. Smaller aquaria can be used for younger specimens but realistically you should not acquire this fish if you cannot house it long term.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>This species requires a lot of swimming space, so it is a good idea to furnish the tank fairly sparsely. Large pieces of bogwood and robust plants are recommended. These are large messy fish and require filtration that is up to the job. External or sump filtration should be considered.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 24–27°C</li><li>pH: 6.5–7.0</li><li>Hardness: up to 15 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>Piscivorous in nature. The diet should consist of meaty foods such as prawn, mussel, lancefish, whitebait and other white fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>Territorial and moderately aggressive. Can be kept in species groups or with other large species such as arowana, stingrays, large cats and other large cichlids. An extremely large tank is required to achieve this with any degree of success. Will eat tankmates small enough to be considered food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>The male is larger and develops a nuchal hump as he matures.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>Limited reports of breeding in the hobby.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/cichla-ocellaris",
      "html": "<p>Due to their large size, Peacock Bass are only really recommended for the experienced fishkeeper that can house them adequately. That said, they are a hardy and robust species that will adapt to a variety of conditions. Peacock Bass are becoming much more available in the hobby and are often seen for sale as cute 5 cm babies, often tempting people into buying a fish they will not be able to care for in the long term. Peacock Bass are also fished as a sport fish.</p>"
    }
  ],
